What happens if you put @Transactional on a private method?
@Service
public class CashService {

    @Transactional
    private void process() {
        // DB operations
    }
}

👉 Spring will ignore it
👉 No transaction will be created
Spring uses AOP proxies for @Transactional.

👉 Key rule:

Transactions are applied only when a method is called through the Spring proxy
Problem 1: Private methods are not proxied
Proxies can only intercept public methods
Private methods are not visible to proxy

👉 So Spring cannot wrap it with transaction logic
Problem 2: Self-invocation (very important)
@Service
public class CashService {

    public void outer() {
        process(); // ❌ direct call (no proxy)
    }

    @Transactional
    public void process() {
        // expected transaction
    }
}

👉 Even though process() is public:

It is called internally
Proxy is bypassed

👉 Transaction will NOT work

🔥 Correct Ways to Use @Transactional
✅ 1. Use on public methods
@Transactional
public void process() {
    // works
}
✅ 2. Call from another bean (important fix)
@Service
public class CashService {

    private final HelperService helper;

    public CashService(HelperService helper) {
        this.helper = helper;
    }

    public void outer() {
        helper.process(); // ✅ goes through proxy
    }
}

@Service
class HelperService {

    @Transactional
    public void process() {
        // transaction works
    }
}
Correct Ways to Use @Transactional
✅ 1. Use on public methods
@Transactional
public void process() {
    // works
}
✅ 2. Call from another bean (important fix)
@Service
public class CashService {

    private final HelperService helper;

    public CashService(HelperService helper) {
        this.helper = helper;
    }

    public void outer() {
        helper.process(); // ✅ goes through proxy
    }
}

@Service
class HelperService {

    @Transactional
    public void process() {
        // transaction works
    }
}
✅ 3. Put @Transactional on entry method
@Transactional
public void outer() {
    process(); // now inside transaction
}
💥 Real Issue You Might Face (Your Project)

You said:

create → update → call MF → rollback if failed

If transaction is on a private/internal method:

Rollback won’t happen ❌
Partial data saved ❌
💬 Interview Answer (Perfect)

“@Transactional does not work on private methods because Spring uses proxy-based AOP, which only intercepts public methods. Also, if a transactional method is called internally within the same class, the proxy is bypassed, so the transaction won’t be applied.”

🚀 Senior-Level Add-on

“To ensure transactions work correctly, the transactional method should be public and invoked through a Spring-managed proxy, typically from another bean or as the entry point of the operation.”

